The current stance and knowledge of physists collectively accord to the principle that electric charge is absolutely conserved. Whilst quantum processes are known to create and destroy the elementary particles, the various tests instantiated to demonstrate this property, by observation of electron decay, yield that if such a decay exists, the mean time would exceed $10^24$ years.

Hence, for a time-static volume $V$, the only perceived way in which the enclosed charge $Q$ may vary with time is if charges move in and out of the the region.
